% lib/text.le — joining, testing and measuring pieces of text, as LE templates.
%
% The rule systems the translators read (Oracle Intelligent Advisor above
% all) build messages, labels and identifiers out of pieces of text: OIA's
% `"Process: " + the parent form + "."`, `Text(the amount)`,
% `Contains(the screen, "&")`. A library, not syntax: include it as a
% resource (a translator copies it beside the program it writes, with
% text.pl), and use its templates like any other.
%
% Each template is implemented by the predicate of text.pl named after it, so
% the LE file carries no Prolog text.
%
% (`*a text* starts with *a part*` is LE's own, language.md §7.)
%
% A number becomes text the way such systems print it: a whole number
% without decimals (70, not 70.0), other numbers with the decimals they need
% (6152.36996634624). A date becomes its ISO form (2026-10-15).

the knowledge base text includes these resources:
    text.pl.

% Each of these gives ONE value for its arguments, so they are declared
% FUNCTIONS (§2.3 of the language reference).
the functions are:
    the joining of *a list* is *a text*.
    the text of *a value* is *a text*.
    the length of *a text* is *a number*.

the templates are:
    *a text* contains *a part*.
    *a text* ends with *a part*.

the knowledge base text includes:

% Every template is answered by text.pl.
